WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:05.500
Jean Valjean zigzagged through the streets, hoping to lose anyone pursuing him.

2
00:00:05.501 --> 00:00:09.126
The moon was full, and Valjean took advantage of that.

3
00:00:09.127 --> 00:00:15.039
He glided along the dark side of every street while keeping a watchful eye on the light side.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.356
He didn't know where he was taking Cosette; he only knew that they must never return to the Gorbeau house.

5
00:00:22.357 --> 00:00:28.451
Valjean tried to reassure himself. Perhaps the man he had seen was not really Javert.

6
00:00:28.452 --> 00:00:36.348
After all, why would Javert be looking for him? Everyone thought that Valjean had died when he fell from the mast of the ship.

7
00:00:36.349 --> 00:00:40.761
Even Valjean had read his own obituary in the newspaper!

8
00:00:40.762 --> 00:00:50.571
Still, strange things had been happening the past few days, and he couldn't take any chances, especially now that his beloved Cosette was in his care.

9
00:00:50.572 --> 00:00:57.999
When a church clock chimed at midnight, Valjean and Cosette were hiding in a doorway on the edge of a square.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.570
Suddenly four large men entered the square and stopped. They appeared unsure about where to go next.

11
00:01:05.571 --> 00:01:09.688
One man pointed in the direction directly opposite Valjean.

12
00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:15.324
But the man who seemed to be the leader turned and pointed straight at Valjean.

13
00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:22.489
Although the leader could not see Valjean and Cosette huddled in the darkness, Valjean could see his face in the moonlight.

14
00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.476
It was definitely Javert.

15
00:01:25.477 --> 00:01:32.151
While the men hesitated, Valjean gathered Cosette in his arms and slipped quietly away.

16
00:01:32.152 --> 00:01:36.556
He decided to cross the Seine River to another part of Paris.

17
00:01:36.557 --> 00:01:42.389
When he reached the other side, he turned and looked back to be sure he hadn't been followed.

18
00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.749
Four shadows were starting to walk across the bridge.

19
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:51.469
Valjean headed into a maze of small streets, turning left and right and left again.

20
00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:56.627
But then he suddenly realized that he was trapped in a long cul-de-sac.

21
00:01:56.628 --> 00:02:02.611
At one end a guard stood silently. At the other was a high brick wall.

22
00:02:02.612 --> 00:02:08.302
Valjean was still a very strong man and he was an expert climber.

23
00:02:08.303 --> 00:02:17.778
If he had been by himself, he could easily have wedged himself into the corner between the wall and a tall building and climbed to the top of the wall.

24
00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:20.508
But how could he take Cosette with him?

25
00:02:20.509 --> 00:02:23.095
Valjean studied the situation.

26
00:02:23.096 --> 00:02:30.841
There was a five-foot pile of stones in the corner, perhaps left there so that beggars would not find it a convenient place to sleep.

27
00:02:30.842 --> 00:02:38.675
But once he climbed the pile, it was still 15 feet to the top of the wall. What he needed was a rope.

28
00:02:38.676 --> 00:02:45.596
He heard the muffled sound of marching as seven or eight soldiers approached, their bayonets glimmering in the moonlight.

29
00:02:45.597 --> 00:02:53.834
Because of that moonlight, the streetlamps had not been lit. Valjean realized the streetlamps held his answer.

30
00:02:53.835 --> 00:03:01.151
A long rope ran down the street connecting all the lamps; the lamplighter used this to lower the lamps to light them.

31
00:03:01.152 --> 00:03:08.302
Quickly Valjean ran to the nearest lamp and cut a length of rope. He wrapped this under Cosette's arms and tied it.

32
00:03:08.303 --> 00:03:16.476
"Stay quiet," he whispered. Then just to make sure she obeyed he added, "Madame Thenardier is after you."

33
00:03:16.477 --> 00:03:22.968
Cosette's eyes grew wide and she stifled a whimper. If only she had Catharine to comfort her.

34
00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:26.619
But she had left her beloved doll behind when they fled.

35
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:33.547
"Put your back against the wall." Valjean took off his shoes, coat, and hat, and threw them over the wall.

36
00:03:33.548 --> 00:03:42.793
Then he wedged himself into the corner, took the rope in his teeth, and began to climb, pushing against the bricks with all his strength.

37
00:03:42.794 --> 00:03:51.452
In no time he was at the top of the wall. He lowered Cosette onto the roof of what looked like a shed and then to the ground.

38
00:03:51.453 --> 00:03:58.190
"Search the cul-de-sac!" roared Javert from the other side of the wall. "He must be here someplace."

39
00:03:58.191 --> 00:04:01.515
Cosette trembled and moved close to Valjean.

40
00:04:01.516 --> 00:04:08.602
After a quarter of an hour, they heard the soldiers leave, and Valjean stepped out of the shed to look for better shelter.

41
00:04:08.603 --> 00:04:15.840
They seemed to be in the middle of a garden. He found a large building, but all the doors and windows were locked.

42
00:04:15.841 --> 00:04:20.674
"What is this strange place in the middle of Paris?" he murmured.

43
00:04:20.675 --> 00:04:29.325
Cosette was sleeping. Suddenly Valjean heard the sound of a bell. He peered from the shed and saw a man limping through the garden.

44
00:04:29.326 --> 00:04:38.071
Every time the man moved, the bell sounded. If this stranger discovered them, the man would surely turn them over to the police.

45
00:04:38.072 --> 00:04:46.055
While Valjean was wondering what to do next, he touched Cosette's hand. It was as cold as ice.

46
00:04:46.056 --> 00:04:52.817
He called her name, but she didn't wake, not even when he shook her. His heart raced. Was she dead?

47
00:04:52.818 --> 00:04:55.802
He bent closer and saw that she was still breathing.

48
00:04:55.803 --> 00:05:01.301
But it was so cold outside. He needed to get her in front of a fire immediately.

49
00:05:01.302 --> 00:05:06.134
Valjean walked straight to the man in the garden and held out a wad of money.

50
00:05:06.135 --> 00:05:11.468
"A hundred francs for you," said Valjean, "if you'll give us lodging for the night."

51
00:05:11.469 --> 00:05:20.214
The man started, blinked several times, and then broke into a grin. "Oh, Mayor Madeleine, you gave me a fright!"

52
00:05:20.215 --> 00:05:27.777
Now it was Valjean's turn to be startled. Mayor Madeleine? He hadn't been called that in what seemed like an eternity.

53
00:05:27.778 --> 00:05:32.857
"How did you get in here?" asked the gardener. "Did you fall from the sky?"

54
00:05:32.858 --> 00:05:37.865
"Who are you and what is this place?" asked Valjean.

55
00:05:37.866 --> 00:05:46.936
"Oh, that's a good one," replied the gardener. "I'm Fauchelevent and this is the convent where you found me employment as a gardener."

56
00:05:46.937 --> 00:05:51.342
"But what are you doing out here so late? And why do you wear a bell?"

57
00:05:51.343 --> 00:05:59.167
"I'm covering some plants so they won't be damaged by the frost. The bell warns the nuns and the convent schoolgirls that I'm near."

58
00:05:59.168 --> 00:06:06.421
"Listen to me, Fauchelevent," Valjean pleaded. "I saved your life once, and now I need you to do the same for me.

59
00:06:06.422 --> 00:06:12.000
Don't tell anyone that you've seen me and don't try to find out anything more about me.

60
00:06:12.001 --> 00:06:15.992
Can you get a girl in front of a fire before she freezes to death?"

61
00:06:15.993 --> 00:06:20.826
Cosette and Valjean were soon safely inside Fauchelevent's cottage.

62
00:06:20.827 --> 00:06:28.159
It was a crime for a man to be at the convent, but Valjean also realized that it could be a haven for him.

63
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.810
If he could get permission to stay, no one would think to look for him there.

64
00:06:33.811 --> 00:06:40.231
"I'm delighted to have a chance to repay you," said Fauchelevent when Valjean asked him about staying.

65
00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:47.802
"You could pretend to be Ultimus Fauchelevent, my brother, and Cosette could be your granddaughter.

66
00:06:47.803 --> 00:06:53.969
I could tell the head of the convent that I'm growing old and tired, and you can help me in the garden."

67
00:06:53.970 --> 00:07:00.961
Soon Valjean was working in the garden, and Cosette traded her mourning clothes for a school uniform.

68
00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:05.294
Valjean packed the black outfit away in a small suitcase.

69
00:07:05.295 --> 00:07:11.208
He could not bear to give up this reminder of the first days they had spent together.

70
00:07:11.209 --> 00:07:17.473
Every day, while taking a break from her studies, Cosette was allowed to visit him for an hour.

71
00:07:17.474 --> 00:07:21.518
In this way they passed several happy years together.

